# RBAC for retrieval

Canonical: `docs/auth.md` and `docs/tenancy.md`. A junior employee must never receive answers derived from documents they cannot open. An employee of another company must never receive answers from this company’s corpus — including documents tagged `all`. This is the core enterprise requirement.

## Enforcement

- Persist `organization_id` and `access_roles` on the `documents` row **and** on every `document_chunks` row.
- On every search/chat/inspect request, filter with the session org **and** the membership `role` / `departments`:

```sql
WHERE organization_id = :current_org
  AND access_roles && ARRAY['employee', 'all']::text[]
```

Apply both conjuncts to **dense and sparse/FTS**. Do not fuse, rerank, or send to the LLM any row that failed.
- `GET /documents` and `GET /documents/{id}/raw` use the same rule. No file bytes without access. Foreign org → same `404` as missing ACL.
- Never “explain why a document was omitted” if that would confirm a restricted or cross-tenant file exists.

## Forbidden

- Filtering only in the prompt (“ignore chunks the user should not see”)
- Client-supplied `access_roles` / `document_ids` / `org_id` / `organization_id` that bypass the token
- Using department tags (`hr`, `all`) as tenant ids
- LLM cache keyed only by roles (must include `org_id`)
- Logging full chunk text of restricted docs in audit trails visible to the same user
- Admin-only debug payloads on production chat endpoints
- Platform operator reading customer corpora (not in product)

## Roles

Membership: `admin` | `manager` | `employee`, plus `departments[]` (e.g. `engineering`, `hr`).
Documents: `access_roles[]` (e.g. `admin`, `hr`, `all`) **inside** the org.
Org-`admin` may manage that org’s KB; retrieval still respects document ACLs. No admin override. No cross-org read.
